  version:
    major: 1
    minor: 0
    patch: 0
    label: 
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds:
    - rid: 5255470237683810306
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: In addition to these buttons, LoaderMono provides more functionality
          to make loading and saving more automatic if that is what you are looking
          for.
    - rid: 5255470237683810315
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '

          The bool loadOnAwake loads the GV on awake. The execution
          order is set to -100. This way it is almost guaranteed that this process
          is the first to be initialized. This value can be changed to be executed
          soonner or later.'
    - rid: 5255470237683810316
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: The bool saveOnDisable saves the GV in the json file when being disabled.
          Depending on the context it is very useful.
    - rid: 5255470237683810317
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: The bool saveOnQuit saves the GV when OnApplicationQuit. This makes
          all data to be saved before closing the application. Very useful if yo
          skip to save data manually.
    - rid: 5255470237683810318
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This a very basic enum where you decide if data should be loaded /
          saved syncronously or asyncronously. It is set to be SYNC at default, althought
          ASYNC is strongly recommended to avoid blocking the main thread. If none
          of the above booleans is checked, this enum does not appear.
    - rid: 7221127993205981360
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Welcome to the load system tool. A tool which will allow you to save
          data without using PlayerPrefs. It's flexible and can store a lot of serialized
          types.
    - rid: 7221127993205981361
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Basically every type. The tool can store in a defined generic<a="Structure">
          structure</a> all primitives. <ui="BadgeInt"/><ui="BadgeBool"/><ui="BadgeFloat"/><ui="BadgeString"/>

          And
          more!'
    - rid: 7221127993205981362
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "For everything! Your game data can be stored in group values. Your
          settings data can be stored too. It allows you to store all your game related
          data. \nIn fact this documentation is stored in GroupValues! You can even
          use this window as a read only window to see your data!\nHave fun!"
    - rid: 7221127993205981363
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This tool requires a medium to advanced knowledge about Unity and
          C#. It is recommended to read this documentation to understand the purpose
          and how this tool functions. It is advised that this tool can get a bit
          complicated in some aspects. However do not despair! If you know the workings
          of PlayerPrefs you can use<a="SimpleGroupValues"> SimpleGroupValues</a>.
          This class works the same as PlayerPrefs with more supported types!
    - rid: 7221127993205981364
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <a="">Group Values</a> is the core of this tool. It is the basic element
          that allows data storage. It acts as an interface to provide structure
          and easy manipulation of data. In fact you can manually set values or add
          information going to the SO through the inspector (but that is not the
          smartest way to do it,see Group Values Editor).
    - rid: 7221127993205981365
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Group values has a lot of different uses and cases. Most of all it
          is a scriptable object in which you can store almost any type of value.
          In scripts you can set and get different values that you can store later
          as persistent data.
    - rid: 7221127993205981366
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Although GroupValues is a flexible SO in which you can store anything
          this SO comes in a defined structure. The smallest element is called entry,
          in which you can store a value. All entries are defined within a field.
          All fields are confined in the GroupValue. That way you can store values
          in a certain area inside the SO.\n<code>\nGroupValues\n|\n|------->Field\n\t|\n\t|------->Entry(value)\n</code>\nExample:\n<code>\nSettings\n|\n|------->SoundSettings\n\t|\n\t|------->Mute(bool)\n\t|------->MasterVolume(float)\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981367
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If you want to create new Grop Values it is as simple as right-clicking
          on your project folder (in your Resources folder preferably) and then going
          to Create->LoadSystem->Group Values. A new instance of GV will be created.
    - rid: 7221127993205981368
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It is really simple. Just click on the bottom '+' button. A new field
          will be created.
    - rid: 7221127993205981369
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Inside a field go click the bottom '+' button. A pop up will appear
          to make you choose a type for that entry value. After you selected one
          you can edit the value and change the entry name. \n\n<a=\"\">IMPORTANT
          NOTE</a>: entry names must be different."
    - rid: 7221127993205981370
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'When you want to get a certain type of value you will ask for the
          name of value that has the same type. So for example, if you want an int
          value with name "speed" to your variable topSpeed the correct way to write
          it is:

          <code>

          GroupValues myValues;

          int
          speed = myValues.GetValue<int>("speed");</code>

'
    - rid: 7221127993205981371
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If the key does not exist it will throw an error. This is important
          to know because <a=""> it can crash the build</a>. It will also throw an
          error if an invalid cast is provided. To prevent crashes it is recommended
          reading about <a="GVEntryReference">GVEntryReference</a> .
    - rid: 7221127993205981372
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'In the same way you can get a value out of the class you can store
          it as well in the scriptable object. Following the same example if you
          want it to asign a value and then store it the correct way to do it is:

          GroupValues
          myValues;<code>

          int speed = 2;

          myValues.SetValue<int>("speed",speed);</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981373
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If the key does not exist it will throw an error. This is important
          to know because<a=""> it can crash the build</a>. It will also throw an
          error if an invalid cast is provided. To prevent crashes it is recommended
          reading about <a="GVEntryReference">GVEntryReference</a> .
    - rid: 7221127993205981374
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "You can dynamically add entries to any Group Values. It is not recommended
          but you can do it if you want. It needs a GVEntry and a field name. Example:\n<code>\nGroupValues
          gv;\nstring valueName=\"DynamicBool\";\n\nGVEntry entry = new GVEntry()\n       
          {\n\n            name = valueName,\n            type = VALUE_TYPE.BOOL,\n           
          customTypeName = \"\",\n            value = new BoolGVValue()\n       
          };\n        entry.value.SetValue(false);\n\n\ngv.TryAddEntry(\"TestingValues\",entry);</code>\nOr
          if you want to add a <a=\"Custom Data\">Custom class</a>:\n<code>\nGroupValues
          gv;\nstring valueName=\"DynamicBool\";\n\nGVEntry entry = new GVEntry()\n       
          {\n\n            name = valueName,\n            type = VALUE_TYPE.CUSTOM,\n           
          customTypeName = \"PlayerData\",\n            value = new StringGVValue()\n       
          };\n        \n        \ngv.TryAddEntry(\"TestingValues\",entry);</code>\n"
    - rid: 7221127993205981375
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If the entry already exists it will log a warning. PLEASE use in <a="">value</a>
          supported GV types. If not used properly unity can get confused with serialization.
    - rid: 7221127993205981376
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'The same way you can create entries dynamically you can remove them.
          Example:

          <code>

          GroupValues gv;

          string
          valueName="DynamicBool";


          gv.TryRemoveEntry(valueName);

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981377
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Warnings will be logged if entry name is not in GV.
    - rid: 7221127993205981378
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If you want to add any other type than the ones shown in the pop up
          entry you can add custom data. For more information read<a="Custom Data">
          Custom</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981379
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It is very important for the user to know that this methods store
          the values inside the SO(Scriptable Object) but it does not mean persistance
          in your data stored. For more information read <a="Loaders">Loaders</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981380
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Underneath Group Values there is a Dictionary that caches all entries
          making all data accesing O(1). This means that Get and Set are really fast
          (almost instantly). You can use bench marking to test it yourself.
    - rid: 7221127993205981381
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Do you want to prevent build crashes? Are you terrified of using a
          string key that you may later change and forget it was being referenced
          by a script and suddenly everything is on fire? Do not worry, <a="">GVEntryReference</a>
          solves that problem.
    - rid: 7221127993205981382
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Very simple, you need a class that needs an access to a certain type
          of value with a certain key. Declare GVEntryReference in your script. Then
          in the inspector go to<a=""> GVEntry->SelectGroupValues->SelectField->SelectEntry</a>.
          Now you have complete access to that entry. No more key using.
    - rid: 7221127993205981383
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Let''s say you want to get your value, it is as simple as writting
          something like this:

          <code>

          [SerializeField] GVEntryReference
          speedReference;

          [SerializeField] float speed;


          speed
          = speedReference.Get<float>();

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981384
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Now let''s say you want to set your value, it is as simple as writting
          something like this:

          <code>

          [SerializeField] GVEntryReference
          speedReference;

          [SerializeField] float speed;


          speed=5;

          speedReference.Set<float>(speed);

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981385
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Whenever a key is changed, in the inspector a warning inside a box
          will be triggered. Also a warning message may be triggered on the console.
          Do not worry. It will auto-assign again. Although manual reassignment is
          strongly recommended to avoid errors. Also, <a="Loaders">Loaders</a> are
          still needed if you want persistance in your data. Setters only apply to
          referenced GroupValues.
    - rid: 7221127993205981386
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Templates are your Group Values readonly data. When building your
          game the preprocessor will set your data to default values. But what if
          you want certain entries with different defult values? Templates provide
          a solution to fix a default value to a Group Values reference.
    - rid: 7221127993205981387
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Templates need a Group Values reference. Once referenced it can access
          to the entry values and set a default value for them. You do not need further
          set up. The preprocessor will automatically set those values as default
          to the referenced Group Values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981388
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Really simple, go to Project Settings->Group Values <a="settings:Project/Load
          System">(click here to see)</a>. Set Use Templates to true.
    - rid: 7221127993205981389
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Let''s say you are creating a fresh template. First of all you need
          to reference any type of Group Values. Then you have to call this method
          to copy all fields and entries in order to create default values for your
          referenced SO. For example:

          <code>

          GroupValuesTemplate
          gvT;

          GroupValues gv;


          gvT.groupValuesReference=gv;

          gvT.SetDefaultValuesInTemplate();//all
          fields and entries are copied in the template

          </code>

          Although
          you can do this, it is not recommended as you are being provided with a
          window that makes your life easier.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981390
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Once you have set a copy inside your template you can then adjust
          values and set them in your Group Values. This practice is not recommended
          as the preprocessor does the job automatically.

          <code>

          //do
          as in previous example

          ...//change data

          gvT.SetDefaultValuesInSO();//all
          fields and entries are set in the referenced Group Values


          </code>

          Again,
          this is not recommended. It is not recommended to call this manually.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981391
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside a Template you should not change anything other than values.
          This means that changing a field name or entry name can lead errors or
          to unexpected behaviour in worst cases. The window editor will not let
          you commit these actions. For more information, read <a="Group Values Editor">Group
          Values Editor</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981392
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <a="Group Values">Group Values </a>is just the tip of the iceberg.
          All data stored in them is not persistent :( .This means that games can't
          store data using only Group Values. However loaders are here to solve this
          problem.
    - rid: 7221127993205981393
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: That is a really good question. When using the editor .json files
          will be stored in the same path as the GV they reference. They will be
          hidden though. When using the build they will be stored in the PersistentData
          path (%USERPROFILE%\AppData\LocalLow).
    - rid: 7221127993205981394
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'This is a C# pure class. It takes a GroupValues reference and can
          convert it to a json file. You can set your GroupValues name and a path
          to start saving your data. However you can not use it as a component. '
    - rid: 7221127993205981395
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'ALoader contains the GV to save/load and acts as the controller of
          a single GV.

          This way you only interact with a loader whenever
          you want to manage data. This way the loader rules over GroupValues and
          provides an abstraction layer.

          <code>


          ALoader

          |

          |

          |->LoadValues/SaveValues/Get/Save----GroupValues

          |->LoadValues/SaveValues--------------JSON

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981396
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "ALoader has different attributes, but most importantly it contains
          a reference to its assigned Group Values. Inside the class you will see:\n<code>\n//\n\n//path
          inside Resources folder\n    [SerializeField] protected string soPath =
          \"Assets/Resources/LoadSystem/SavedFiles/\";\n//Name without extension\n   
          [SerializeField] protected string baseName = \"Game\";\n\n    protected
          string soName => baseName + \".asset\";\n    protected string jsonFileName
          => \".\" + baseName + \".json\";\n\n//Project settings\n    [ReadOnly][SerializeField]
          EncryptionMethod encryptionMethod = EncryptionMethod.None;\n    [ReadOnly][SerializeField]
          string password = \"\";\n\n//The core, GroupValues reference\n    [SerializeField]\n   
          [ExposedScriptableObject]\n    protected GroupValues values;\n//\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981397
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "ALoader provides the same methods as Group Values (as it provides
          an additional abstraction layer to the SO). This means that it comes with
          Get and Set and work the same.\n<code>\nALoader loader;//loader has speed
          set as 5\nint speed = 0;//initialize variable\nspeed = loader.GetValue<int>(\"speed\");\nspeed
          += 1; \nloader.SetValue<int>(\"speed\",speed);// value is set to 6\n\n\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981398
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'ALoader can load data from a json for you. If the path and name are
          set up correctly it will read the data from the json file and pour it inside
          the referenced GroupValues. It also returns GV loaded.

          <code>

          //before
          reading make sure at the start to have synchronized data

          ALoader
          loader;

          loader.LoadData();


          int speed = loader.GetValue<int>("speed");


          </code>

          There
          is also async loading:

          <code>

          ALoader loader;

          await
          loader.LoadValuesAsync();</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981399
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Not assigning a name or name that does not exist in Resources folder
          will not find GV and will log an error message every time you load data.
    - rid: 7221127993205981400
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'ALoader can save data to a json as well. If the set up is correct.
          It will perform the action without any problems.

          <code>

          //you
          can do this before closing app for example

          ALoader loader;

          int
          speed=5;

          loader.SetValue<int>("speed",speed);

          loader.SaveData();

          </code>

          There
          is also async saving:

          <code>

          ALoader loader;

          await
          loader.SaveValuesAsync();</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981401
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Not assigning a name or name that does not exist in Resources folder
          will not find GV and will log an error message every time you save data.
    - rid: 7221127993205981402
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'LoaderMono comes as your useful component and implements ALoader
          to save the day(and your data). This component allows you to set, get,
          load and save data in runtime as ALoader does. Yippie!

          It works
          the same as ALoader as it provides an abstraction layer. As mentioned before
          you can set your GroupValues name and click on AutoFindPathByName to have
          everything set up. '
    - rid: 7221127993205981403
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<ui="Save"/><ui="Load"/><ui="Reset"/>

          These buttons do
          the as what their name implies. You can save and load through these buttons
          or set to default without using <a="Group Values Editor">Group Values Editor</a>
          or scripting.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981404
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'It is up to the user to decide when to load or save data.

          In
          project settings you can set the encryption method and change the password(For
          more information read  <a="Encryption">Encryption</a> or <a="Project Settings">Project
          Settings</a>). '
    - rid: 7221127993205981405
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Loaders will throw exceptions when path is not correctly set, Group
          Values is not found or when there is no Group Values to save. Be careful
          and keep these things in mind.
    - rid: 7221127993205981406
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Have you ever tried to move a file .asset to a different project?
          Even though the SO script is in the project unity will remove all data.
          This tool comes with a solution. You can export your GroupValues.asset
          to a json, xml or csv file to then import such file in other projects.
          This means you can recover Group Values and reuse them for future projects!
          Yay!
    - rid: 7221127993205981407
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: By selecting <a="">Tools->LoadSystem->Export Group Values</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/Export
          GroupValues"> (click here to see)</a> you will open the export window.
          Select from the Assets folder any type of Group Values. Then select the
          file format, click 'Export' and save it anywhere.
    - rid: 7221127993205981408
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: By selecting <a="">Tools->LoadSystem->Import Group Values</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/Import
          GroupValues"> (click here to see)</a> you will open the import window.
          Select your file with the same file extension. Then select somewhere inside
          Assets to save the new Group Values (it is recommended to save it within
          a Resources folder).
    - rid: 7221127993205981409
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This tool allows an easier way to add more entries or more fields,
          make changes to entry values and much more. It is the best way to manage
          your Group Values as when applying changes it will also save it to json.
    - rid: 7221127993205981410
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Go to <a="">Tools->LoadSystem->Group Values Editor</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/Group
          Values Editor"> (click here to see)</a>. Do not be overwhelmed by the amount
          of buttons we will cover them one by one.
    - rid: 7221127993205981411
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: The first thing you will see is that GroupValues is in color blue.
          This is a toolbar that tells you that you are currently working on Group
          Values. You can change it to edit Templates too.
    - rid: 7221127993205981412
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="OpenSettings"/>This button is pretty much self-explanatory. It
          opens the LoadSystem<a="Project Settings"> project settings</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981413
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="RefreshList"/>Just in case your freshly created GV(Group Values)
          does not show on the list below you can click on this button to make sure
          it appears.
    - rid: 7221127993205981414
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside the list it provides it sets the GV that you will work on.
    - rid: 7221127993205981415
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: All buttons stated appear for both workplaces. However similarities
          end here and each class posseses its own buttons with equivalent behaviour.
    - rid: 7221127993205981416
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="CreateGroupValues"/>It creates a new GV SO wherever you want.
    - rid: 7221127993205981417
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="DeleteSelected"/>Deletes the current working GV
    - rid: 7221127993205981418
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="ResetAll"/>Sets all GV values to default. It does not keep in
          mind templates.
    - rid: 7221127993205981419
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It is a toggle that when activated allows you to see the values that
          the template will set on your working GV.
    - rid: 7221127993205981420
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="CopyFromCurrent"/>Copies the current working GV and lets you
          save it wherever you want
    - rid: 7221127993205981421
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="Apply"/>This is the most important button as it saves all changes
          and creates the json file.
    - rid: 7221127993205981422
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="ResetToDefaults"/>Sets current working GV  to default values
    - rid: 7221127993205981423
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: As stated before Templates have buttons with similar behaviour.
    - rid: 7221127993205981424
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="CreateTemplate"/>It creates a new Template wherever you want.
    - rid: 7221127993205981425
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="DeleteSelected"/>Deletes current working template.
    - rid: 7221127993205981426
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="ResetAll"/>Resets all Templates to default values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981427
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="TemplateFromGV"/>This has the same behaviour as the method SetDefaultValuesInTemplate.
          It copies the referenced GV fields and entries.
    - rid: 7221127993205981428
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="TemplateToGV"/>This has the same behaviour as the method SetDefaultValuesInSO.
          It sets the referenced GV fields and entries with the template values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981429
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="Apply"/>Saves changes in the template.
    - rid: 7221127993205981430
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="ResetToDefaults"/>Sets all current working Template values to
          default.
    - rid: 7221127993205981431
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: <ui="BadgeCustom"/>Do you feel like you don't have enough types to
          save? Do you want to make your own clean custom sets of data instead of
          making a thousand entries? The type Custom has you covered. With it you
          can set your class as custom and chuck it inside GV.
    - rid: 7221127993205981432
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "As everything in this tool it is really simple. You only have to
          add the property [CustomSettingData(\"Name\")] on top of your class declaration.
          For example:\n<code>\n\n//\n[CustomSettingData(\"PlayerStats\")][Serializable]
          //Serializable property is a must\npublic class MyPlayerStats\n{\n\tpublic
          Vector3 position;\n\tpublic int speed;\n}\n//\n</code>\nThen it will appear
          in the registry. Create a new entry and set your custom data class."
    - rid: 7221127993205981433
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'If you want to set or get a custom data class you need to save or
          load the entire class. For example using <a="GVEntryReference">GVEntryReference</a>:

          <code>

          MyPlayerStats
          stats;

          GVEntryReference statsReference;


          //First
          read loaded data

          stats=statsReference.Get<MyPlayerStats>();


          //Then
          change data and save

          stats.speed=5;

          statsReference.Set<MyPlayerStats>(stats);

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981434
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "The answer is yes. You can add more than one custom data level. \nFor
          example you can do this:\n<code>\n\n[CustomSettingData(\"GameStats\")][Serializable]\npublic
          class MyGameStats\n{\n\tpublic int time;\n\tpublic string CurrentChapter;\n\n\tpublic
          MyPlayerStats playerStats;//MyPlayerStats is custom too :)\n}\n\n</code>\n<a=\"\">HOWEVER,
          DO NOT SELF REFERENCE OR HAVE TWO CLASES REFERENCE EACH OTHER</a>.\nIf
          you want to avoid stack overflow I recommend thinking very well before
          adding custom data inside custom data. User is advised."
    - rid: 7221127993205981435
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "It also allows Quaternion, Color, Vector4, Bounds and AnimationCurve
          serialization among others. But most importantly it allows Lists made of
          primitive types. It will not serialize anything with a gameObject reference.
          \nVariables inside CustomData can also be referenced with GVEntryReference.
          When referencing CustomData an extra path will appear to drill down the
          class. "
    - rid: 7221127993205981436
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Do you want all the features that<a="Custom Data"> Custom Data</a>
          provides but you do not want to add the tag [CustomSettingData("NameofCustom")]
          to every single class you need to save? <a="">GroupValuesWrapper</a> has
          you covered.
    - rid: 7221127993205981437
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "It is quite simple actually. First of all your class must be serializable.
          Add [Serializable] on top of your class if the tag does not exist. For
          example:\n<code>//\n[Serializable] //Serializable property is a must\npublic
          class MyPlayerStats\n{\n\tpublic Vector3 position;\n\tpublic int speed;\n}\n//</code>\nThen
          declare wrapper variable in your mono component:\n<code>\nGroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>
          stats;\n</code>\n<ui=\"BadgeCustom\"/>\nThis will add it to your Custom
          collection as another valid Custom entry."
    - rid: 7221127993205981438
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "The wrapper in order to be initialized properly needs a GroupValues
          reference and a key. \nYou can skip initialization as the wrapper has static
          Load and Save. But it the wrapper has to be declared somewhere in order
          to access a wrapped class. For example:\n<code>\npublic GroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>
          stats;\npublic GroupValues gv;\npublic string key;\n\npublic void Start()\n{\n\tstats=new
          GroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>(gv, key);\n}\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981439
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "With a similiar behaviour as Custom Data the entire class is loaded
          in the referenced entry. Here are two different ways to use the wrapper:\nInitializing
          the wrapper.\n<code>\npublic MyPlayerStats playerStats;\npublic GroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>
          stats;\npublic GVEntryReference reference;\n\npublic void OnEnable()\n{\n\tstats=new
          GroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>(reference.GroupValues,\n\t\treference.EntryKey);\n}\n\n[Button(\"SetStats\")]\npublic
          void SetStats()\n{\n\n\tplayerStats=stats.Load();\n\n\tplayerStats.speed=5;\n\n\tstats.Save(playerStats);\n}\n</code>\nUsing
          static methods.\n<code>\npublic MyPlayerStats playerStats;\n//stats must
          be declared on this script or elsewhere but a wrapper of MyPlayerStats
          type must be somewhere.\npublic GroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats> stats;\npublic
          GVEntryReference reference;\n\n[Button(\"SetStats\")]\npublic void SetStats()\n{\n\n\tplayerStats=GroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>.Load(reference.GroupValues,\n\t\treference.EntryKey);\n\n\tplayerStats.speed=5;\n\n\tGroupValuesWrapper<MyPlayerStats>.Save(reference.GroupValues,\n\t\treference.EntryKey,playerStats);\n}\n</code>\nBoth
          ways are valid. However, initializing the wrapper requires less writting
          when trying to access data."
    - rid: 7221127993205981440
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "The entry for the wrapper will appear as an entry in Group Values
          once declared on your script. Then create a new entry with Custom type
          and select the wrapped class. \nAs wrappers are grouped with Custom Data
          the variables within them can also be referenced with GVEntryReference."
    - rid: 7221127993205981441
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If you are curious about how much size do all your GroupValues take
          this tool gives you exactly that. It shows how much space does the .asset
          and the .json take and sums it all. Go to <a=""> Tools/LoadSystem/GroupValues
          Size Inspector</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/GroupValues Size Inspector">
          (click here to see)</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981442
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Watching your values change in the inspector is really nice. However,
          people that play your game want to see those values INSIDE the game, ****
          the inspector. That is why UIGVElement is presented to you as a solution
          to see the values changed in runtime in UI elements. It is a component
          that you can add to a UI element(toggle, drawer,slider or input field)
          and loads and saves an entry value perfectly.
    - rid: 7221127993205981443
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "It is a bit tricky. However this script has been simplified to require
          0 user modification.There are two methods in which ui can access Group
          Values. \n\nThe first one is through a window provided by this tool and
          the second one needs a little bit of programming. Both are valid and window
          usage is encouraged to prevent errors.\n\nFor the second method in the
          conceptual field you need a manager (a SettingsManager for example) that
          registers itself in a service locator. Once registered, each ui element
          can access the locator and ask for the registered instance. With that instance
          it can Get and Set values as a normal GV."
    - rid: 7221127993205981444
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Open the window in <a="">Tools->LoadSystem->UI Settings Elements</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/UI
          Settings Elements"> (click here to see)</a>.

          Yet another window,
          I know. But this one is as simple as everything provided by this tool.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981445
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This window creates ui elements based on prefabs. Some examples are
          provided to you. <a="">Click on Prefab Defaults</a>, it will show a list
          of different types of UI elements and their prefab assigned. This way you
          can assign your own prefabs or use the provided examples. Prefabs will
          be saved for further use.
    - rid: 7221127993205981446
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside the window click on the '+' button.  Set a name to the element.
          Pick an entry and the value and type of ui element will be auto-assigned.
          The prefab will also be auto-assigned based on the type of UI. If you prefer
          other type of prefab you can manually change it. Everything can be manually
          changed. Assign a Canvas parent Then click on create.
    - rid: 7221127993205981447
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: I know that this method might sound bizarre so let me guide you with
          the steps needed to link ui and GV.
    - rid: 7221127993205981448
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Your settings manager must implement IValuesContainer and register
          itself on the static class ValuesContainerLocator. For example, this set
          up should do:\n<code>\npublic class SettingsManager : Monobehaviour, ISettingsContainer\n{\n\tpublic
          GroupValues settingsValues;\n\t[SerializeField] List<UIGVElement> uiElements;\n\n\tpublic
          void Awake()\n\t{\n\t\tsettingsValues=GetComponent<LoaderMono>().LoadValues();\n\t\tSettingsContainerLocator.Register(this);\n\t}\n\tpublic
          void  SetValue<T>(string key,T value)\n    \t{\n        \t\tif (settingsValues
          == null) return;\n        \t\t\tsettingsValues.SetValue<T>(key, value);\n       
          \t\t\n    \t}\n    \tpublic T GetValue<T>(string key)\n    \t{\t\n\t\t\n\t\tif
          (settingsValues == null) return null;\n        \t\t\treturn settingsValues.GetValue<T>(key);\n   
          \t}\n}\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981449
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Then you can access your list of UIGVElement and asign values in
          your manager. Following the same example you can set:\n<code>\npublic class
          SettingsManager : Monobehaviour, ISettingsContainer\n{\n\tpublic GroupValues
          settingsValues;\n\t[SerializeField] List<UIGVElement> uiElements;\n\t//...
          interface functions\n\n\t//Initialize interface with stored values in settingValues\n\tpublic
          void LoadData()\n\t{\n\t\tforeach(var uiEl in uiElements)\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tuiEl.LoadData();\n\t\t}\n\n\t}\n\n\t//When
          closing ui save as json with loader\n\tOnCloseUI()\n\t{\n\t\tGetComponent<LoaderMono>().SaveData();\n\t}\n\n}\n</code>\nThis
          gives you more control about when to load data(default Behabiour in UIGVElement
          must be disabled). Following this pattern you can create your own interfaces
          and locators for other type of cases.\n"
    - rid: 7221127993205981450
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'If your UIGVElement is created and left with default values it auto-detects
          the ui element and on enable it will load the value. <a="">IMPORTANT NOTE</a>:
          Key value must be the same as in GV. Also if no key value is provided it
          will try with gameobject name. In addition, key value must be within the
          entry name of the same GV referenced in the SettingsManager.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981451
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 
    - rid: 7221127993205981452
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "<code>[Button(\"Button\")]</code>\nInstead of using context menu
          to test functions on the inspector button provides a button that can execute
          parameterless methods.\nFor example:\n<code>\n[Button(\"Hello World\")]\npublic
          void Log()//methods must be public in order to show the button\n{\n\tDebug.Log(\"Hello
          World\");\n}\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981453
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "<code>[ExposedScriptableObject]</code>\nAllows you to see all atributtes
          from an Scr iptable Object inside a MonoBehaviour. It also allows writing
          on it.\nFor example to show GroupValues ALoader uses:\n<code>\n[SerializeField]\n[ExposedScriptableObject]
          \nprotected GroupValues values;\n</code>\n"
    - rid: 7221127993205981454
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[UniqueReference]</code>

          This is a special type
          of tag. It constrains an object to only be referenced by a single class.

          For
          example, this is used in Templates:

          <code>

          [UniqueReference]

          public
          GroupValues groupValuesReference;

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981455
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "<code>[Showif(\"boolIstrue\")]</code>\nIt only shows a variable if
          a condition is met. It also works for more than one condition. \n<code>[Showif(\"bool1Istrue\",\"bool2Istrue\")]</code>\nThe
          more used case scenario I personally use is:\n<code>\n[Header(\"Debugging\")]\n[SerializeField]\ndebug=true;\n\n[SerializeField]\ndebugGame=true;\n\n[SerializeField]\ndebugScene=false;\n\n[SerializeField]\n[ShowIf(\"debug\",\"debugGame\")]//This
          will appear on Inspector\n//debug and debugGame = true\n\ndebugGameSpeed=5;\n\n[SerializeField]\n[ShowIf(\"debug\",\"debugScene\")]//This
          will not appear on Inspector \n//debug and debugScene = false\n\ndebugTickSceneSpeed=10;\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981456
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[ReadOnly]</code>

          Makes a serialized field as a
          read only value. You can not edit that value. For example, if you want
          to see the value of a variable you can set it as this:

          <code>

          [ReadOnly]
          public float tickTimer=0;

          </code>

'
    - rid: 7221127993205981457
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[CustomLabel("Overrided propfield name")]

          [CustomLabel]

          </code>

          It
          allows you to override a property name instead of using the one it has
          assigned. Adding it without arguments hides the name completely.

          For
          example:

          <code>

          [SerializeField,CustomLabel("SPEED")]float
          speed; //the inspector will show SPEED

          [SerializeField,CustomLabel]float
          maxSpeed;//the inspector will NOT show a name assigned to maxSpeed

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981458
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside a <a="Custom Data">custom</a> class you can set special attributes
          depending on what behaviour will have your values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981459
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[DontSave]</code>

          If for whatever reason you do
          not want a value to be saved because it is temporal you can mark it with
          this attribute.

          <code>[DontSave] float speedTimer=0;</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981460
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "<code>[GVReadOnly]</code>\nMakes a field read-only in both the inspector
          and via GVEntryReference. The value is still serialized and loaded normally
          \u2014 it is only protected from manual edits.\n<code>[GVReadOnly] public
          string readonlyStr= \"This is read only\";</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981461
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[GVMin(n)] [GVMax(n)]</code>

          Adds a min or max restriction
          to numeric values. It ONLY works on scalars.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981462
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[GVRange(min,max)]</code>

          Adds a range restriction
          to numeric values. It ONLY works on scalars.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981463
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "<code>[WriteOnce]</code>\nIt only writes a value one time, can be
          useful for storing a once per app open value. It can be useful for storing
          keys or in-game passwords randomly generated. Click on the R button to
          reset and be able to write once more.\n<code>[WriteOnce] \nfloat puzzlepassword;\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981464
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[WriteN(n)]</code>

          It can write a value n times,
          if for whatever reason you need this it is provided. Click on the R button
          to reset and be able to write once more.

          <code>[WriteN(3)]

          int
          countDown;

          public int __countDownWriteCount; // companion field
          required to track write count

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981465
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: The interfaces provided by this tool are examples of how your scripts
          can interact with Group Values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981466
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "This interface was first mentioned in <a=\"UIGVElement\"> UIGVElement</a>
          and is the perfect example when using managers and GV. Inside of it you
          can find:\n<code> public void SubscribeToSettingsChange(Action onChange);\npublic
          T GetValue<T>(string key);\npublic void SetValue<T>(string key,T value);</code>\nAnd
          bellow the interface an static class with similar name:\n<code>public static
          class SettingsContainerLocator</code>\nTo make your manager work register
          your interface on Awake.\n<code> SettingsContainerLocator.Register(this);//Manager</code>\nNow
          classes that need your manager to find their respective values can find
          it easily with:\n<code>SettingsContainerLocator.Get()//Smaller class</code>\nNow
          when asking for a value the script does not need a GV reference and it
          is decoupled.\n<code> float speed=SettingsContainerLocator.Get().GetValue<float>(\"speed\");//Smaller
          class</code>\nYou can use this piece of code to set up a manager:\n<code>\npublic
          class ManagerSettings : Monobehaviour, ISettingsContainer\n{\n\tpublic
          GroupValues gv;\n\tpublic UnityEvent onSettingsChange;\n\n\tpublic void
          Awake()\n\t{\n\t\tSettingsContainerLocator.Register(this);\n\t}\n\n\tpublic
          void SubscribeToSettingsChange(Action onChange);\n\t{\n\t\tonSettingsChange.AddListener(onChange);\n\t}\n\n\tpublic
          T GetValue<T>(string key)\n\t{\n\t\treturn gv.GetValue<T>(key);\n\t}\n\n\tpublic
          void SetValue<T>(string key,T value)\n\t{\n\t\tgv.SetValue<T>(key,value);\n\t\tonSettingsChange.Invoke();\n\t\t//GV
          has been changed, notify all observers\n\t}\n}\n</code>\nThis interface
          gives guidance on what your code architecture can look like when using
          GV. This way is clean and avoids referencing GV on every script."
    - rid: 7221127993205981467
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "This interface can apply to all those scripts that need a certain
          value from a GV.\nIt comes with:\n<code>public void SubscribeToValuesChange();\npublic
          void OnValuesChange(); </code>\t\nInside of the first method you can subscribe
          to UnityEvents that are triggered when a value is changed. On the second
          one you decide what values are being read when values change. \nThis means
          that ILoaderUser and ISettingsContainer can be used together.\nYou can
          use this piece of code to use a manager with this type of interface:\n<code>\npublic
          class LoadSpeed : ILoaderUser\n{\n\tISettingsContainer container;\n\tpublic
          float speed;\n\n\tpublic void Init()\n\t{\n\t\tcontainer=SettingsContainerLocator.Get();\n\t\n\t\tif(container!=null)\n\t\t\tcontainer.SubscribeToSettingsChange(OnValuesChange);\n\t\t\t//Set
          method for when UnityEvent is invoked\n\t}\n\n\tpublic void OnValuesChange()\n\t{\n\t\tspeed=container.GetValue<T>(\"speed\");\n\t}\n}\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981468
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: These are examples of how you can use GV and having some listenners
          that react when values have changed.
    - rid: 7221127993205981469
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This script provides a full fledged example of what you can achieve
          with GV and a good design in architecture. Go check it out in<a=""> LoadSystem/Examples</a>
    - rid: 7221127993205981470
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This script also provides a full fledged example. Go check it out
          in<a=""> LoadSystem/Examples</a>
    - rid: 7221127993205981471
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "As you can see, these scripts act as observers or listenners however
          you want to call them. When being initialized they look for a container
          instance. It is seen on:\n<code> SubscribeToValuesChange();</code>\nIf
          a container is found, then it subscribes to its UnityEvent.\n<code>container
          = SettingsContainerLocator.Get();\n\n        if (container != null)//if
          there is a settingsContainer, subscribe OnValuesChange method\n           
          container.SubscribeToSettingsChange(OnValuesChange);</code>\nThen, whenever
          a UnityEvent is invoked these listenners will pick their own values and
          apply changes.\nThis means that this tool gives more purpose than just
          storing data.  It makes it easier to pick up values stored and avoids spaguetti
          code."
    - rid: 7221127993205981472
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This tool provides all in all a lot of functionality. But as a developer
          you do not require all tools at all times. That is why you can go to <a="settings:Project/Load
          System">Project Settings</a> and change a lot of behaviours.
    - rid: 7221127993205981473
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: In here you can change some settings
    - rid: 7221127993205981474
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Allows you to save in a subfolder in persistent path all json files.
    - rid: 7221127993205981475
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Creates a backup copy of json file. If original file is corrupted
          or not found a new GV will be made by this backup and it will make a copy
          of this backup as the new original file. Read more in <a="Backups"> Backups</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981476
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Apply created <a="Group Values Template">templates</a> in project
          over referenced Group Values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981477
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Allows selection between three different methods of encryption. Aes
          256_Hmac Sha 256 is the safest among the three. You can always have none
          if you do not mind keeping your json files safe. This encryption methods
          are global and all loaders are updated when changes are made.
    - rid: 7221127993205981478
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This password is what encrypts and deciphers json files if a method
          other than none is used. Pressing <a=""> Generate New Salt</a> creates
          a new password.
    - rid: 7221127993205981479
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Adds to scriptting symbols the symbol LOG_LOADSYSTEM to stop annoying
          loggings from the tool. You can use it as well.
    - rid: 7221127993205981480
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If you did not notice there are buttons with ? that lead you to this
          documentation. If you want to disable those buttons click on the toggle.
    - rid: 7221127993205981481
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It contains all data in reference to debugging Group Values. For more
          information read about <a="Debugging"> Debugging</a>.
    - rid: 7221127993205981482
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Allows using the Debug Overlay Window.
    - rid: 7221127993205981483
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Which key is pressed in order to make the window appear. <a=\"\">IMPORTANT
          NOTE</A>: If using the new Input System keep in mind to use in Active Input
          Handling  \u201CBoth\u201D. Otherwise the key will not work."
    - rid: 7221127993205981484
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Allows user to edit values. It is very useful depending on the situation.
    - rid: 7221127993205981485
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Sets Time.timeScale to 0 when openning the window. Keep it in mind
          when using it as it can mess with time if other script is also messing
          with timeScale.
    - rid: 7221127993205981486
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Not the most important aspect inside the tool but keeps windows pretty
          and organized. \nThere are four default themes to choose from. Minimal
          uses the same colors as unity. Although my favourite is OceanBlue.\nThere
          are buttons to edit the current theme, create a new one or regenerate defaults
          (if you have erased them, you monster). They come with a bunch of settings
          that you can adjust if you want a deep level of customization. Also text
          size is customizable. "
    - rid: 7221127993205981487
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Are you afraid that your data is not safe by exposing all your GV
          information? Are you afraid that your data can be modified by clients?
          You are right ! This is why this tool ensures safety by providing different
          types of encryption. '
    - rid: 7221127993205981488
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Really easy, go to Project Settings->Group Values <a="settings:Project/Load
          System">(click here to see)</a>. Then select your preferred encryption
          method. A key will be randomly generated.
    - rid: 7221127993205981489
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: As it name suggests it is a more user friendly class to save all your
          data. If you are already using Group Values or intend to use it I recommend
          skipping this.
    - rid: 7221127993205981490
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It is really simple as it works exactly as PlayerPrefs. You do not
          have to create a Scriptable Object nor do you have to create entries. If
          you are not familiarized with it here are some examples.
    - rid: 7221127993205981491
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'You only need a key and a value. Do not fear generics! If you want
          to set a value for example, speed you only have to write this line of code:

          <code>float
          speed=5;

          SimpleGroupValues.Set("speed",speed);

          //SimpleGroupValues.Set<float>("speed",speed);
          works as well

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981492
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "For this, you only need a key. As simple as this:\n<code>float speed=SimpleGroupValues.Get(\"speed\");\n//SimpleGroupValues.Get<float>(\"speed\",speed);
          \n"
    - rid: 7221127993205981493
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Yes, absolutely there is a way to watch all your saved data(Unlike
          PlayerPrefs (\xAC_\xAC) ). Go to <a=\"\">Tools->LoadSystem->SimpleGroupValues
          Viewer</a><a=\"action:Tools/LoadSystem/SimpleGroupValues Viewer\"> (click
          here to see)</a>."
    - rid: 7221127993205981494
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: SimpleGroupValues provides a ready to go solution for storing data
          in with way more types than PlayerPrefs and gives the user the option to
          see their created data. However, it does not incorporate all the advantages
          that GroupValues brings to the table. It can not use <a="Group Values Template">
          Templates </a> and do not give the user the control in general that Group
          Values gives. Group Values allows you to make a more structured data storage
          and is way more better.
    - rid: 7221127993205981495
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Keep in mind what entry key and what value you are requesting when
          setting or getting a value. You CAN NOT get a value you HAVE NOT set.
    - rid: 7221127993205981496
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: This is a tool that you may require when working with bigger data
          amounts. It can provide lists of entries of the same type. It can count
          how many type entries there are, return a list of keys for a value type,
          make sums, calculate average (if type is numeric).
    - rid: 7221127993205981497
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Here is a list of all the methods that come with this static class.
          \nThese can be used to calculate statistics about your stored data.\n\nMost
          method take always as argument GroupValues. You can also pass the <a=\"\">field
          name</a> if you only need to know the result from a certain field."
    - rid: 7221127993205981498
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Returns a list of type T. Example:

          <code>List<int>speeds=GroupValuesUtility.GetAll<int>(gv);</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981499
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Counts all entries of type type. Returns an integer.Example:

          <code>int
          speedCount=GroupValuesUtility.CountAll<int>(gv);</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981500
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Sums all numeric type data. Can sum vectors up to Vector3 (VectorsInt
          excluded).Example:

          <code>

          float sumSpeeds=GroupValuesUtility.SumAll<float>(gv);</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981501
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Gets the max numeric type data. Example:

          <code>float MaxSpeed
          = GroupValuesUtility.Max<float>(gv);'
    - rid: 7221127993205981502
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Gets the max numeric type data. Example:

          <code>float MinSpeed
          = GroupValuesUtility.Min<float>(gv);'
    - rid: 7221127993205981503
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Calculates the numeric type average. Can calculate average of vectors
          up to Vector3 (VectorsInt excluded). Example:

          <code>Vector3
          averagePoints=GroupValuesUtility.Average<Vector3>(gv);'
    - rid: 7221127993205981504
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Calculates the numeric type Variance. Can calculate variance of vectors
          up to Vector3 (VectorsInt excluded). Example:

          <code>float speedVariance=GroupValuesUtility.Variance<float>(gv);'
    - rid: 7221127993205981505
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Returns the square root of Variance<T>. Example:

          <code>float 
          speedStd=GroupValuesUtility.StdDev<float>(gv);</code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981506
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Calculates the percentages of numeric types.\nFor example if you
          have three different entries of type int. Entry1=300, Entry2=70 and Entry3=20.
          In total they sum 390. So for Entry1 its percentage is 300/390 * 100 and
          so on.\nUse FormatPercentages to see full information. Example:\n<code>GroupValues
          gv;\nvar typedist = GroupValuesUtility.CalculatePercentages(gv);\n\nif
          (typedist.Count > 0)\n{\n            Debug.Log($\"Type dist     \u2192
          {GroupValuesUtility.FormatPercentages(gv)}\");\n}</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981507
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Returns most used words in your string entries. Use FormatMostUsedWords
          to see full information. Example:\n<code>  \nGroupValues gv;\nvar strings
          = GroupValuesUtility.GetAll<string>(gv);\nif (strings.Count > 0)\n           
          Debug.Log($\"Words   \u2192 {GroupValuesUtility.FormatMostUsedWords(gv,
          topN: 5)}\");\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981508
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Returns the percentages of all entry types used in your GroupValues.
          Use FormatTypeDistribution to see full information. Example:\n<code>\nGroupValues
          gv;\nvar typedist = GroupValuesUtility.TypeDistribution(gv);\n\nif (typedist.Count
          > 0)\n{\n            Debug.Log($\"Type dist     \u2192 {GroupValuesUtility.FormatTypeDistribution(gv)}\");\n}</code>"
    - rid: 7221127993205981509
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Returns the median of numeric types. Can calculate median of vectors
          up to Vector3 (VectorsInt excluded). Example:

          <code>

          GroupValues
          gv;

          float medianSpeed=GroupValuesUtility.Median<float>(gv);

          </code>


'
    - rid: 7221127993205981510
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'Returns a normalized list of values between 0 and 1 (as floats) based
          on min and max value. Can calculate normalization of vectors up to Vector3
          (VectorsInt excluded). Example:

          <code>

          GroupValues
          gv;

          List<float> speedsNorm=GroupValuesUtility.Normalize<float>(gv);

          </code>'
    - rid: 7221127993205981511
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Are you having troubles because some value is being changed? Maybe
          you want to set a certain value without using the inspector. Maybe you
          are inside the build and don't want to show all data in the ui, but you
          want to see certain values being changed. How about you check out the debugging
          window.
    - rid: 7221127993205981512
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: By enabling it in project settings and pressing a key (F1 by default)
          a window will pop up. In there you can select any GroupValues you wish
          to see or modify values.
    - rid: 7221127993205981513
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: It is fairly simple, go to <a="">Project Settings->Group Values</a>
          <a="settings:Project/Load System">(click here to see)</a>.You also need
          to activate <a="">Development Build on Build Settings</a>. Go to Enable
          Overlay and set it to true. You can set the key you prefer. Whether you
          want to have values only be read. In addition you can freeze time when
          the window is open.
    - rid: 7221127993205981514
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'What if you want a scalable system when adding or removing entries
          while keeping user data untouched? Migrating correctly your GroupValues
          can answer to that question. But first you need to know how it works. Keep
          in mind that migration is only needed once you have created your first
          build. '
    - rid: 7221127993205981515
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Inside <a="Group Values Editor">Group Values Editor</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/Group
          Values Editor"> (click here to see)</a> when adding or removing an entry
          or field a suggested bump message will appear. Accept suggested change
          or manually change it.
    - rid: 7221127993205981516
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: User values stored in the build are preserved and new or deleted data
          is synchronized.
    - rid: 7221127993205981517
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: KEEP IN MIND to update (Template from GV) your <a="Group Values Template">templates</a>
          as well as they can <a="">override</a> your changes.
    - rid: 7221127993205981518
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: The backup system protects player save data from corruption. When
          enabled, every time data is saved a backup copy is written first, then
          the main file is written. This means there is always a valid previous version
          available.
    - rid: 7221127993205981519
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: 'On load, the system tries the main file first. If it is corrupted
          or missing, it falls back to the backup automatically and restores the
          main file from it. If both are missing or corrupted, default values from
          the SO are used and both files are recreated.

          Save order:

          <code>

          1.
          Write backup (.backup.json)

          2. Write main (.json)

          </code>

          This
          means if the game crashes mid-save, the backup always holds the last valid
          state.'
    - rid: 7221127993205981520
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Go to <a="settings:Project/Load System">Project Settings -> Load System</a>
          and enable Use Backups. All LoaderMono components in the project will be
          updated automatically.
    - rid: 7221127993205981521
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Each GroupValues has a version number (major.minor.patch and an optional
          label). This version is saved in the JSON file and compared against the
          SO version at load time. If they differ, a <a="Migrations">migration</a>
          is triggered automatically to merge player data with the new structure.
    - rid: 7221127993205981522
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: At the bottom of the <a="Group Values Editor">Group Values Editor</a>
          window there is a version bar showing the current version of the selected
          GroupValues. You can edit Major, Minor, Patch and Label directly from there.
          Changes are applied when you click Apply.
    - rid: 7221127993205981523
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: When clicking Apply after making structural changes (adding or removing
          entries or fields), the editor will suggest a version bump automatically.
          You can accept the suggestion or keep the current version. It is strongly
          recommended to accept it so that the migration system can detect the change
          and update player saves correctly.
    - rid: 7221128000120815616
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "The final encryption key is derived by combining two elements: a
          project-wide password salt (set in Project Settings) and a device key unique
          to each installation. The device key is a randomly generated file stored
          in persistentDataPath the first time the game runs. From that point on
          it is always reused, making the encryption unique per user and per project.\nThis
          means two players with the same build cannot read each other's save files,
          and a save file cannot be moved to another device.\n<a=\"\">IMPORTANT NOTE</a>:
          on PC the device key persists across reinstalls as long as persistentDataPath
          is not manually deleted. On mobile platforms, uninstalling the app deletes
          persistentDataPath along with the device key \u2014 if the player reinstalls,
          a new key is generated and any previously encrypted save files become unreadable."
    - rid: 7221128000120815635
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[SaveAs("OtherName")]</code>

          Works the same as CustomLabel
          but for variables inside custom data.

          <code>[SaveAs("SPEED")]float
          speed;</code>'
    - rid: 7221128000120815660
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: If you are eager to use this tool to it's fullest potential you can
          jump right into and create your first Group Values in <a="Getting Started">Getting
          Started</a>. What are you waiting for?
    - rid: 7221128000120815661
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Right click in your Resources folder and go to Create->LoadSystem->Group
          Values. A new GroupValues asset will be created. Open <a="Group Values
          Editor">the editor</a><a="action:Tools/LoadSystem/Group Values Editor">
          (click here to open)</a> and select your new asset. Add a field by clicking
          '+ Add Field' and then add a few entries inside it with '+ Add Entry'.
          Set some values and click Apply.
    - rid: 7221128000120815662
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "No loaders needed yet. Create a new class to try. Just reference
          the SO directly:\n<code>\n[SerializeField] GroupValues myValues;\n\nvoid
          Start()\n{\n    int speed = myValues.GetValue<int>(\"speed\");\n    myValues.SetValue<int>(\"speed\",
          speed + 1);\n}\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221128000120815663
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Add a LoaderMono component to any GameObject in your scene. Set the
          name to match your GroupValues asset. Press Save in the inspector to verify
          the JSON file is created. In code in the class you created before(or a
          new one), call LoadData() on Awake and SaveData() before closing the app:\n<code>\n[SerializeField]
          LoaderMono loader;\n\nvoid Awake()\n{\n    loader.LoadData();\n}\n\nvoid
          OnApplicationQuit()\n{\n    loader.SaveData();\n}\n</code>"
    - rid: 7221128000120815664
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: "Instead of using string keys in code, declare a <a=\"GVEntryReference\">GVEntryReference</a>.
          In the inspector select your GroupValues, then the field, then the entry.
          No more risk of typos or broken references when renaming keys:\n<code>\n[SerializeField]
          GVEntryReference speedReference;\n\nvoid Update()\n{\n    float speed =
          speedReference.Get<float>();\n    speedReference.Set<float>(speed + 1);\n}\n</code>\n"
    - rid: 7221128000120815665
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: In a build, save files are stored in persistentDataPath. Make sure
          LoadData() is called at startup and SaveData() is called before the app
          closes. If you want encryption go to <a="settings:Project/Load System">Project
          Settings -> Load System</a> and select an encryption method before building.
          If you want to add or remove entries after shipping read <a="Migrations">Migrations</a>
          and <a="Versioning">Versioning</a>.
    - rid: 7933554929974640993
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: '<code>[GVHide]</code>

          As the name suggests hides values
          on the inspector inside a Group Values.

          <code>[GVHide] UnityEvent
          onSpeedChange;

          </code>

'
    - rid: 7933554929974640994
      type: {class: StringGVValue, ns: GVLoadSystem.Core, asm: LoadSystem}
      data:
        value: Makes text be read more easily. For example if you store a variable
          with the name "hasBeenRead" it will transform it into "Has Been Read".
          It works the same as the Unity Inspector. If it is bothering you can deactivate
          it.
